  got these ones here which sound way  better than the built-in LG's so much of  course that makes sense and I'll just go  ahead and link those for you guys down  in the video description if you're  interested now let's get into what makes  this display worth that $6,000 price tag  the image quality and I would actually  argue way more than $6,000 yes it is  6-game meaning it has just over 20  million pixels whereas the LG 5k has a  little bit under 15 million now that may  sound like you're getting a lot more  detail but since the display itself is a  larger 32 inches compared to 27 inches  you're still getting the same 218 pixels  per inch which Apple considers red and  equality both look extremely good and at  normal viewing distances you can't see  the pixels unlike a 4k display that's  about as large as these two the LG 5k  display can output a really solid 500  nits of brightness but it's not enough  for Apple to consider HDR capable even  though a lot of other brands mark  there is displays a supporting HDR which  some of them actually have brightness as  low as 315 it's the pro display XDR on  the other hand can sustain a massive  1000 nits of brightness across the  entire display at the same time and up  to a shocking 1600 nits in certain areas  of the screen when working with HDR  content however when you're not viewing  or working with HDR both the LG 5k and  the pro display XDR will max out at  around 500 nits since you don't really  need the extra brightness this is one  area I wish Apple gave us more room to  play with on the xcr display both  displays have some brightness shift when  viewing at extreme angles and there also  is some color shift but the LG 5k is  much worse as you can see by the white  balance while the XDR stays very similar  reflectivity is fairly similar unless  you buy the nano-coated XDR for $1,000  more which pretty much eliminates  reflections while maintaining colors and  sharpness which is an amazing  achievement by Apple one 